{"name":"Rare White Jade","hex":"#e8e9cc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rare","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Very uncommon; scarce.","Small in number (but not unusual); infrequent; sparse."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A scarce or uncommon item."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rare"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"},{"term":"jade","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A semiprecious stone, either nephrite or jadeite, generally green or white in color, often used for carving figurines.","A bright shade of slightly bluish or greyish green, typical of polished jade stones."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a grayish shade of green, typical of jade stones."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jade"}],"entries":[{"title":"Nephrite","blocks":[{"text":"Nephrite is a variety of the calcium, magnesium, and iron-rich amphibole minerals tremolite, actinolite or ferro-actinolite (aggregates of which also make up one form of asbestos). The chemical formula for nephrite is Ca2(Mg, Fe)5Si8O22(OH)2. It is one of two different mineral species called jade. The other mineral species known as jade is jadeite, which is a variety of pyroxene. While nephrite jade possesses mainly grays and greens (and occasionally yellows, browns, black or whites), jadeite jade, which is rarer, can also contain blacks, reds, pinks and violets. Nephrite jade is an ornamental stone used in carvings, beads, or cabochon cut gemstones. Nephrite is also the official state mineral of Wyoming."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Nephrite_jordanow_slaski.jpg/330px-Nephrite_jordanow_slaski.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nephrite","wikidata":"Q138979"},{"title":"Jade","blocks":[{"text":"Jade is an umbrella term for two different types of decorative rocks used for jewelry or ornaments. Jade is often referred to by either of two different silicate mineral names: nephrite, or jadeite. Nephrite is typically green, although it may be yellow, white or black. Jadeite varies from white or near-colorless, through various shades of green, to lavender, yellow, orange, brown and black. Rarely it may be blue. Both of these names refer to their use as gemstones, and each has a mineralogically more specific name. Both the amphibole jade (nephrite) and pyroxene jade are mineral aggregates (rocks) rather than mineral species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Jadestein.jpg/330px-Jadestein.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jade","wikidata":"Q175089"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"},{"title":"Shades of green","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color green may differ in hue, chroma or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a green or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Shades_of_Green.svg/330px-Shades_of_Green.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_green","wikidata":"Q19847180"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/%27Mutton_fat%27_jade_for_sale_at_Khotan_Jade_Market.jpg/500px-%27Mutton_fat%27_jade_for_sale_at_Khotan_Jade_Market.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/%27Mutton_fat%27_jade_for_sale_at_Khotan_Jade_Market.jpg/500px-%27Mutton_fat%27_jade_for_sale_at_Khotan_Jade_Market.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A'Mutton_fat'_jade_for_sale_at_Khotan_Jade_Market.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Large_mutton_fat_jade_displayed_in_Hotan_Cultural_Museum_lobby.jpg/500px-Large_mutton_fat_jade_displayed_in_Hotan_Cultural_Museum_lobby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Large_mutton_fat_jade_displayed_in_Hotan_Cultural_Museum_lobby.jpg/500px-Large_mutton_fat_jade_displayed_in_Hotan_Cultural_Museum_lobby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALarge_mutton_fat_jade_displayed_in_Hotan_Cultural_Museum_lobby.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Amphibole_2_-_Nephrite_Jade_Basic_calcium_magnesium_iron_silicate_Lander_Coun